# Breaking the Limits of Dijkstra: A New Frontier in Shortest Path Algorithms

The **single-source shortest path (SSSP)** problem is one of the cornerstones of graph theory and computer science. For decades, **Dijkstra’s algorithm** has been the go-to method when edge weights are non-negative, offering a balance of efficiency and elegance.

Traditionally, the runtime of Dijkstra’s algorithm depends on the priority queue implementation with complexity of  \(O(m + n \log n)\),
here \(n\) is the number of vertices and \(m\) the number of edges.

---

## A New Algorithmic Breakthrough

Recently, researchers proposed a **new algorithm** that challenges the long-standing dominance of Dijkstra. The key highlight:

\[
\text{Time complexity: } O\big(m \cdot \log^{2/3} n\big)
\]

This is strictly better asymptotically than Dijkstra’s \(O(m + n \log n)\) bound, especially for **sparse graphs** where \(m\) is closer to \(n\).

---
But in order to make the algorithm efficient we need to identify the maximum value of \(m\) such that the proposed approach outperforms the dijistra.

\[
n \log n + m > m \log n,
\]

Accordingly, m should becomes

\[
m < \frac{n \log n}{\log^{2/3} n - 1}.
\]

In such cases, we could define the density of a graph with respect to the number of edges. Interestingly, 
the density (i.e., number of edges divided by number of nodes) drops below 1% at around 1,000 nodes.


This is generally reasonable for road networks, where not every node (i.e., intersection) is connected to every other node via road segments. 
In practice, most intersections connect to only a handful of nearby roads, making real-world road networks sparse graphs. 
This sparsity is exactly why algorithms with improved logarithmic factors, like the new SSSP method with O(m log^(2/3) n), 
can yield significant performance gains over Dijkstra, especially as the network scales to tens or hundreds of thousands of nodes.



